          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>You and a colleague are performing CPR on a 4-year-old child who collapsed on the ward. After delivering five initial rescue breaths, which compression-to-ventilation ratio should you use?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>30:2</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>30:2 is the ratio for a lone rescuer managing a child, or the standard adult ratio. With two healthcare providers, the correct child CPR ratio is 15:2.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>15:2</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. With two providers, child CPR (age one year to puberty) uses a 15:2 ratio. The higher ventilation frequency reflects the fact that paediatric arrest is most often caused by respiratory failure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>3:1</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>3:1 is the compression-to-ventilation ratio for Newborn Life Support, where one breath is coordinated after every three compressions. A 4-year-old child requires a 15:2 ratio with two rescuers.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>5:1</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>5:1 is not a current resuscitation ratio for any age group. The correct two-provider ratio for a child aged one year to puberty is 15:2.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the age-specific ratios: adults and lone-rescuer children use 30:2; two-provider children use 15:2; newborns use 3:1 with coordinated breaths."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. In two-provider paediatric CPR, the 15:2 ratio increases ventilation frequency — critical when the underlying cause of arrest is usually respiratory failure."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
