          "label": "<p>During Advanced Life Support, a patient remains in ventricular fibrillation afte</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During Advanced Life Support, a patient remains in ventricular fibrillation after the third shock. Which drug combination should be administered at this point?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Atropine 1 mg IV and amiodarone 300 mg IV</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Atropine is used for symptomatic bradycardia, not for shockable cardiac arrest rhythms. The correct combination after the third shock in VF/pVT is adrenaline 1 mg IV and amiodarone 300 mg IV.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adrenaline 1 mg IV and amiodarone 300 mg IV</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. After the third shock in a shockable rhythm, give adrenaline 1 mg IV and amiodarone 300 mg IV. Adrenaline is then continued every 3–5 minutes throughout the resuscitation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adrenaline 1 mg IV and lidocaine 100 mg IV</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lidocaine is an older alternative anti-arrhythmic but is not the first-line agent in current ALS guidelines. The recommended combination after the third shock is adrenaline 1 mg IV with amiodarone 300 mg IV.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adrenaline 0.5 mg IV and amiodarone 150 mg IV</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These are incorrect doses. The standard adult doses after the third shock are adrenaline 1 mg IV and amiodarone 300 mg IV. Accurate dosing is critical during cardiac arrest.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "After the third shock in VF/pVT: give adrenaline 1 mg IV and amiodarone 300 mg IV, then resume compressions immediately. For non-shockable rhythms, adrenaline 1 mg IV is given as soon as access is established."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. In the shockable pathway, both adrenaline and amiodarone are given after the third shock. Adrenaline continues every 3–5 minutes; a second dose of amiodarone (150 mg) may follow after the fifth shock."
                }
              ],
