          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>After return of spontaneous circulation in an unconscious adult, the monitor sho</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>After return of spontaneous circulation in an unconscious adult, the monitor shows an SpO₂ of 100% on high-flow oxygen. What is the most appropriate next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Continue high-flow oxygen to maximise tissue oxygenation during recovery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Maintaining unnecessarily high oxygen levels (hyperoxia) is associated with worse neurological outcomes after cardiac arrest. Oxygen should be titrated down to a target SpO₂ of 94–98%.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Titrate oxygen downward to target an SpO₂ of 94 to 98 percent</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Post-ROSC guidelines recommend titrating supplemental oxygen to an SpO₂ of 94–98%. Hyperoxia generates reactive oxygen species that exacerbate reperfusion injury to the brain.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Discontinue all supplemental oxygen immediately</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Abruptly removing supplemental oxygen risks hypoxaemia, which is equally harmful. The correct approach is gradual titration, targeting an SpO₂ of 94–98%.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Increase oxygen flow to maintain SpO₂ above 99% for neuroprotection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>An SpO₂ persistently at 100% represents hyperoxia. This is associated with worse, not better, neurological outcomes after cardiac arrest. The target is 94–98%.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Post-ROSC oxygen management is counterintuitive: the target is 94–98% SpO₂. Hyperoxia (SpO₂ persistently at 100%) worsens cerebral reperfusion injury. Titrate down gradually — do not maximise."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. After ROSC, more oxygen is not better — titrate to 94–98% SpO₂ to avoid both hypoxaemia and hyperoxia-mediated neurological injury."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
